## Why the baseline film is mediocre

The evidence discipline in that document is genuinely excellent and I'd keep all of it. The craft is not. The short version:

It **mistakes rigour for craft** — count what it measures (LUFS, sample counts, colour matrices) against what it says about why a viewer would care; the ratio is about forty to one. And it admits it: *"No human listening test was performed."*

The **voice was chosen without listening.** Kokoro `bm_george`, a small local model, selected on availability. The document concedes the alternatives got sample files but no comparative test. The narrator is the largest single quality variable in a narrated film and it was picked by default.

The **pacing method is inverted.** Copy was written, measured, then time-stretched up to 1.14× to fit a fixed scene grid. Every downstream problem — the 0.235s-per-segment shortfall accumulating to 146.7 seconds, the sample-padding fix, the caption drift — is a symptom of fitting the performance to the container instead of cutting picture to the voice.

The **resolution chain destroys the argument.** Captured at 1600×720, delivered at 1080p, pushed to 1.34×. That's a 1600px source shown at roughly 2.6× on every emphasis shot. For a product whose pitch is *readable evidence*, soft text on every zoom is the film contradicting itself.

And there is **no sound design layer at all** — the document says so outright. No transitions, no UI ticks, no impact on the seal. That absence separates amateur from professional more reliably than the music does.

## Where I disagreed with you

**Epic orchestra, partly.** A full trailer bed under this product sounds like a token launch, and Marque's voice is *measured, not asserted* — bombast argues against the narrator. What I've specced instead earns its scale: sub-bass and one piano note in the cold open, sparse ostinato through the working section, low brass under the Charter, and the full swell held back until the mainnet proof at 2:21, where it lands as a payoff.

**And the device nobody uses: 1.5 seconds of total silence at the word "zero."** Every other submission will have wall-to-wall music. Yours stops dead on the most important number in the product. That will make the film feel more expensive than anything else in the document and it's free.

**Film the light theme.** Your screenshots are all dark, which collapses *light for looking, dark for doing* into nothing. In the film, everything is daylight and the screen dims to the cockpit exactly once — at the Charter. One transition, and it becomes the most memorable frame you have.

